  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Seattle turned this into a rout before the second inning ended.",
    "what_this_means": "The Mariners scored in every inning but one and turned a ballgame into a 19-run statement. Gage Jump lasted two innings, the bullpen bled runs across 6.1 frames, and the offense managed five hits and seven strikeouts in a night that got away early and never came back. Shea Langeliers drove in the only run that mattered. By the time the eighth inning arrived, the damage was finished. This was not a collapse. It was a dismantling from the first pitch onward.",
    "what_to_watch": "An 18-run deficit by the fifth inning is not a game waiting to be won. It is an evening you survive and move past. The Mariners methodically piled on. Multiple scoring sequences in the first, second, fourth, fifth, seventh, and eighth. While the A's scratched out a single RISP conversion and nothing else. Seth Johnson, Joey Estes, Yunior Tur, and Carlos Cortes combined to give up 10 runs across 6.1 innings. The structure of the bullpen did not hold. Seattle refused to let them off the hook. One run and five hits is not the kind of night that builds anything forward. They needed something early from Jump. They got nothing.",
